<article class="detail-page"><a class="detail-back" href="/insights/">← Back to insights</a><div class="section-label">Cost & Margins</div><h1>Vendor Rationalisation: How We Reduced a Client's Vendor Base from 187 to 64 — and Saved ₹3.2Cr</h1><div class="detail-content modal-body"><p>This is the story of a real engagement — an auto components manufacturer with ₹115Cr revenue and 187 active vendors, none with formal contracts.</p><h4>The Diagnosis</h4><p>When we mapped spend across all 187 vendors, we found: the top 20 vendors accounted for 78% of total spend. 43 vendors had received less than one order in the past 12 months but remained "active." 31 vendor pairs were essentially duplicates — supplying the same category with minor differences in spec.</p><h4>The Rationalisation Process</h4><p>Step 1: Identify the strategic vendors — those whose products are critical, unique, or where switching cost is high. These become your "strategic tier" — typically 15–25 vendors. Step 2: Consolidate transactional spend — for commodity categories, consolidate to 2–3 vendors per category and negotiate volume-based pricing. Step 3: Formalise contracts — every remaining vendor gets a contract with defined pricing, quality specs, lead times, and SLA penalties. Step 4: Create a vendor scorecard and review quarterly.</p><h4>The Result</h4><p>187 vendors became 64. Average price reduction across renegotiated categories: 8.4%. Annual saving delivered: ₹3.2Cr — from procurement alone, before touching any other cost line.</p></div><a class="btn-primary" href="/#contact">Request a Discovery Call →</a></article>
</Layout>
